**Week 5 — Keypoint Detection and Animation Generation**

### Work completed

Studied the main animation-generation stage of the project.

Examined how the driving video is read frame by frame using ImageIO.

Reviewed how the FPS of the driving video is obtained and used when creating the final output video.

Studied the conversion of the source image and driving-video frames into 256 × 256 arrays before they are passed to the model.

Examined the `make_animation()` function and its main inputs:

* Source image
* Driving video frames
* Generator
* Keypoint detector
* Relative keypoint displacement
* Adaptive movement scale

Studied the purpose of the keypoint detector in identifying motion information from the driving sequence.

Examined the `relative` option and understood that it allows the motion from the driving video to be applied relative to the source image.

Reviewed the `adapt_movement_scale` option and how it can adjust the movement according to the scale of the objects in the source and driving inputs.

### Error/problem faced

A potential issue during animation generation is inconsistent input quality between the source image and driving video.

If the source image or video contains unsuitable cropping, unclear subject information or inconsistent dimensions, the generated animation may not produce the expected result.

Another possible issue is that animation generation can require significant processing time depending on the model and driving-video length.

### Resolution

The source image and driving frames were kept at the required 256 × 256 resolution before being passed to the model.

The relative keypoint displacement and adaptive movement scale settings were reviewed to understand how they affect the generated movement.

The processing stages were also examined individually so that problems could be identified during input preparation, model loading or animation generation.

### Dataset/project picture

Generated animation comparing the original source image with the driving-video motion applied by the First Order Motion Model.

### Learning

I learned that the model does not simply copy the driving video. Instead, motion information is extracted from the driving sequence and applied to the appearance of the source image. The keypoint detector and generator therefore play important roles in transferring motion from one input to another.
